<div class="sheet">

  <!-- ============ COVER ============ -->
  <section class="cover" id="cover" style="padding:0;">
    <img src="/images/resources/gen-exit-playbook-cover.jpg" alt="Gen eXit Playbook — New Strategy for Gen Xers Wanting An Earlier 9–5 Exit" style="width:100%; display:block;">
    <div style="padding:26px 48px 36px;">
      <div class="issue-stamp" style="display:inline-block; margin-bottom:16px;">The Playbook · Subscriber Edition</div>
      <div class="cover-meta">
        <span>By Corey Koehler</span>·<span>Redefining Retirement</span>·<span>Class of '90</span>
      </div>
      <div class="cover-tagline">"We all got taught defense. Save, save, save. Nobody mentioned there was a whole other half of the field."</div>
      <p class="dek" style="margin-bottom:4px;">A different way to think about getting out of the 9-to-5 a little sooner — for those of us who did the math on "just save more" and came up short. Five things that worked for me, and the one little thing underneath all of them.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ HELLO / BIO PAGE ============ -->
  <section class="bio" id="hello">
    <div class="bio-label">RedefiningRetirement.io</div>
    <h2>Hello!</h2>
    <div class="bio-photos">
      <img class="portrait" style="width:260px; height:auto; border-radius:0;" src="/images/corey-throwback.jpg" alt="Corey Koehler">
    </div>
    <div class="name">Corey Koehler</div>
    <p>Hey, I'm Corey Koehler. Kay-ler. Foundry worker, then 17 years parked behind a CAD station as a mechanical draftsman, singer-songwriter on the weekends, family guy, 80s latchkey kid. Class of '90.</p>
    <p>Nothing on that list says "runs his own thing." I sure didn't think so. But somewhere along the way I stumbled into a handful of ways to make money on my own terms — and pieced together an exit that didn't take winning the lottery or a 401(k) miracle. This is the stuff I wish somebody had handed me 20 years ago. No system. Just what worked.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ CASSETTE DIVIDER — slot 1 ============ -->
  <div class="cassette-divider"><img src="/images/cassette-mess-rewind.jpg" alt=""></div>

  <!-- ============ TABLE OF CONTENTS ============ -->
  <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
    <div class="toc-label">The Game Plan</div>
    <h2>Contents</h2>
    <div class="toc-readtime">About a 7-minute read</div>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#offense"><span>The Other Half of the Field</span><span class="dots"></span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#plan"><span>Five Things That Worked</span><span class="dots"></span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#list"><span>It All Ran on a List</span><span class="dots"></span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#proof"><span>What That Looks Like, Years Later</span><span class="dots"></span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#next"><span>Start Your List Today</span><span class="dots"></span></a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <!-- ============ CHAPTER I: WHY OFFENSE ============ -->
  <section class="chapter" id="offense">
    <div class="chapter-label">Chapter I</div>
    <h2>The Other Half of the Field</h2>
    <p>Here's the retirement plan we all got handed: pick a company, grind for 40 years, shove money in the 401(k), and cross your fingers it's enough at 65.</p>
    <p>For a lot of us in Gen X, that plan quietly stopped working. Pensions? Gone. Social Security? Who knows. And "just save more" isn't really a plan — it's a wish with a calculator next to it.</p>
    <p>So I started thinking about it like a football game. Stick with me here.</p>
    <p>There's two sides to the ball. Saving money is defense. Making money is offense. Most people play defense their whole life — squeeze the budget, clip the coupons, save a little harder — and then can't figure out why it still feels like they're one bad month from trouble.</p>
    <div class="pull">You don't win a game on defense alone. Ask any Vikings fan.</div>
    <p>In my house, my wife runs the defense. She watches what comes in and what goes out, and she's a lot better at it than I am. My job's always been offense — finding new ways to get the ball moving and put a little more on the board.</p>
    <p>That's all "playing offense" really means. Instead of only protecting what you've got, you build a few little ways to bring money in that don't lean on one boss or one company. Takes the pressure off the whole thing.</p>
    <p style="font-weight:600;">Here's how I'd start.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ CHAPTER II: THE 5 THINGS ============ -->
  <section class="chapter" id="plan">
    <div class="chapter-label">Chapter II</div>
    <h2>Five Things That Worked</h2>

    <div class="step">
      <div class="step-num">Play 1</div>
      <h3>Know Your Players</h3>
      <p>Before you run anything, you gotta know who's on your roster. And your roster is you.</p>
      <p>Sit down and take stock of three things: the stuff you can already do without thinking about it, the 20-some years of work experience you've piled up, and the dumb hobbies you actually enjoy. Somewhere in that pile is something other people will pay for. Usually more than one something.</p>
      <p style="font-weight:600;">You can't call the plays if you don't know your players.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="step">
      <div class="step-num">Play 2</div>
      <h3>Pick One Way In</h3>
      <p>There's really only three ways to get paid without a boss. Pick whichever one fits where you're at right now — you can add the others later.</p>
      <ul class="case-list">
        <li><strong>Sell your time</strong> — consulting, freelancing. Fastest way to actual money. You're renting out 20 years of know-how you already have.</li>
        <li><strong>Sell something you make once</strong> — a course, a template, an ebook. More work up front, but you build it one time and it can sell for years while you sleep.</li>
        <li><strong>Build an audience</strong> — a newsletter. The slow one. You show up, people gather, and eventually you've got something worth a whole lot. Pays the least at first, highest ceiling by a mile.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Most folks end up running two or three of these eventually. Just don't try to start all three on a Tuesday.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="step">
      <div class="step-num">Play 3</div>
      <h3>Try It Before You Bet On It</h3>
      <p>Whatever you pick — do not quit your job to "go all in." That's not brave. That's a good way to scare your spouse and torch your savings.</p>
      <p>Start small. On the side. On a weekend. Let it be bad for a while.</p>
      <p>It's a whole lot cheaper to find out an idea flops on a Saturday afternoon than to find out after you bet the 401(k) on a hunch.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="step">
      <div class="step-num">Play 4</div>
      <h3>Build the One Thing You Actually Own</h3>
      <p>This is the play you run no matter which way in you picked. Start collecting email addresses. Day one. Even before you've got anything to sell.</p>
      <p>Here's the hill I'll die on. Social media isn't yours — you're renting. The algorithm changes, your reach gets choked, the platform has a bad year, and poof, the audience you spent three years building isn't yours anymore.</p>
      <p>An email list is the one thing you actually own. Nobody can take it. I've still got people on my list from music sites I ran over a decade ago, and they still open my stuff.</p>
      <div class="callout">Start the list on day one. The list is the asset. Everything else gets built on top of it.</div>
      <p>This is where a tool like Beehiiv comes in — and it's the thread running through the back half of this thing. Hang tight, I'll get there.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="step" style="border-bottom:none;">
      <div class="step-num">Play 5</div>
      <h3>Run It Back</h3>
      <p>Once one little thing is working, don't stop. Listen.</p>
      <p>Your list and your bank account start telling you what people are actually stuck on. That's your next play, handed to you for free.</p>
      <p>That's how one income stream quietly turns into three, and how a weekend side project turns into the thing that takes the pressure off the whole retirement question.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ CASSETTE DIVIDER — slot 2 ============ -->
  <div class="cassette-divider"><img src="/images/cassette-roadtrip-mixtape.jpg" alt=""></div>

  <!-- ============ CHAPTER III: IT ALL RAN ON A LIST ============ -->
  <section class="chapter" id="list">
    <div class="chapter-label">Chapter III</div>
    <h2>It All Ran on a List</h2>
    <p>Let me show you how this actually went for me, because I promise I didn't draw it up this way. I backed into the whole thing.</p>
    <p>Started around 2004 with two dinky little blogs — one about fantasy football, one about marketing music. I was a draftsman making about $40K a year, zero business background, typing away about stuff I cared about at night after the kids went down.</p>

    <figure class="fig">
      <img src="/images/resources/pb-fantasy-football-2008.avif" alt="Corey's fantasy football blog from 2008">
      <figcaption>My fantasy football blog, way back around 2008. Where the whole thing started.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>The football blog pulled $300–500 a month in ad money. Two posts a week, and only during football season. The music one had a few $500 days selling guitar courses as an affiliate — and I'm not a guitar teacher, I just liked the courses. Small money. But it was mine, and it told me something was there.</p>
    <p>Then came the part I never saw coming. I started grabbing email addresses. And this quiet little loop kicked in:</p>
    <div class="pull">A post brought in subscribers. The emails pulled folks back to the site. Google saw the traffic, figured the site mattered, and ranked it higher — which brought in more readers. Round and round.</div>
    <p>I don't have a slick name for it. The list just made everything else work better. The blogs, the affiliate checks, the consulting that came along way later — all of it sat on top of that one little list.</p>
    <p>And that same "eh, let's try it" streak is what let me prove I could land clients cold on Upwork years later. Hundred percent job score and everything.</p>

    <figure class="fig">
      <img src="/images/resources/pb-upwork-100.avif" alt="Corey's Upwork profile showing 100% job success">
      <figcaption>100% job score on Upwork — proof you can build a reputation from a dead stop.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>The wild part? People who got on my music lists more than ten years ago still open my emails. Still buy stuff. Try getting that out of a follow on some app.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ CHAPTER IV: THE PROOF ============ -->
  <section class="chapter" id="proof">
    <div class="chapter-label">Chapter IV</div>
    <h2>What That Looks Like, Years Later</h2>
    <p>Now, I'm not showing you this to brag. There are people out there with way bigger numbers than mine — this isn't that. I'm showing you because it's proof of what a regular guy can put together on the side, one little list at a time.</p>
    <p>One of the things I'm building right now is just a simple affiliate setup with Beehiiv — the same tool I use to send my own newsletter. Here's the running total:</p>

    <figure class="fig">
      <img src="/images/resources/pb-beehiiv-earnings.jpg" alt="Beehiiv affiliate lifetime earnings of about six thousand dollars">
      <figcaption>A little over $6K and climbing, mostly while I'm off doing other things.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>But honestly, the number on the chart isn't the part that gets me. This is — my actual inbox. One little "You earned a commission!" email after another, showing up while I'm busy doing something else entirely:</p>

    <figure class="fig">
      <img src="/images/resources/pb-beehiiv-inbox.jpg" alt="Corey's inbox full of Beehiiv commission notification emails">
      <figcaption>My real inbox. Every one of these landed on its own.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>None of this took blowing up my life. I built it right next to a regular job, with three skills anybody can pick up: write a little, market a little, stay curious. And with the AI tools we've got now, the learning curve is a sliver of what it was for me.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ CASSETTE DIVIDER — slot 3 ============ -->
  <div class="cassette-divider"><img src="/images/cassette-boombox.jpg" alt=""></div>

  <!-- ============ BACK MATTER / CTA ============ -->
  <section class="backmatter" id="next">
    <div class="chapter-label">What's Next</div>
    <h2 style="font-size:30px; margin-bottom:22px;">You Need a List. But First, You Need to Know What It's For.</h2>
    <p>If you walk away from this whole thing with one idea, make it this one: you're playing offense now.</p>
    <p>But before you start a newsletter, sell a thing, or freelance your first job — you need to know which one is actually yours. Picking the wrong one isn't free. It costs you the months (and the motivation) most people burn building toward something they quit on by month four.</p>
    <p>That's the one piece I didn't put in this playbook. Not because I'm holding out on you — it's a process, not something I can hand you in a chapter.</p>

    <div class="pull">Pick a direction first. Build the list second. In that order, every hour you put in compounds toward something — instead of starting over twice.</div>

    <!-- ============ COMPASS CTA ============ -->
    <div class="promo">
      <div class="promo-tag">Your Next Move</div>
      <h3>Find Your Direction: The Gen eXit Compass</h3>
      <p>If you already grabbed it on the signup page, go finish your North Star — that's the answer to "what's it for." If you skipped it, it's still waiting for you, and it walks you through the exact same three paths (sell your time, sell a product, build an audience) — just with a process for picking the right one instead of guessing.</p>
      <a href="https://redefiningretirement.io/products/the-gen-exit-compass" class="promo-cta">Get The Gen eXit Compass →</a>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:26px;">Once you've got your direction, the list is the easy part. Hit reply to my Sunday email either way and tell me what you're building. I read every one. No pitch, no upsell, no funnel — I'm just nosy.</p>

    <p class="script" style="font-size:22px; color:var(--gej-accent);">— Corey</p>

    <div class="actions">
      <a href="https://www.redefiningretirement.io" class="btn primary">Come Hang at RedefiningRetirement.io</a>
      <button class="btn" onclick="window.print()">Save / Print This Playbook</button>
    </div>

    <div class="colophon">
      <span>Gen eXit Playbook · Subscriber Edition</span>
      <span id="delivered-date"></span>
      <span>RedefiningRetirement.io</span>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>
</div>

